    content: `<p class="lead">Every practical temperature scale in everyday use — Celsius, Fahrenheit — is relative. 0°C was arbitrarily defined as the freezing point of water; 0°F as the coldest brine mixture Fahrenheit could make. For thermodynamics — the physics of heat and energy — a relative scale creates mathematical problems: equations involving temperature ratios require a true zero, not an arbitrary one. The Kelvin scale provides this. Defined from absolute zero, Kelvin allows thermodynamic equations to be written correctly, enables understanding of temperature in terms of molecular energy, and is the SI base unit of temperature.</p>
<h2>How Kelvin Relates to Celsius and Fahrenheit</h2>
<p>K = °C + 273.15. Conversely, °C = K − 273.15. 0 K = −273.15°C = −459.67°F. 273.15 K = 0°C = 32°F. 373.15 K = 100°C = 212°F. 310.15 K = 37°C = 98.6°F. Note: Kelvin does not use the degree symbol — it is written as "300 K" not "300°K." This convention distinguishes it from relative scales and reflects its status as an absolute quantity, not a position on an arbitrary scale.</p>
<h2>Where Kelvin Is Used in Science</h2>
<p>Thermodynamics: all equations involving temperature ratios (e.g., Carnot efficiency = 1 − T_cold/T_hot) require absolute temperatures in Kelvin. Astrophysics: stellar surface temperatures (the Sun's surface is ≈5,778 K) and cosmic background radiation (2.73 K). Cryogenics: liquid helium boils at 4.22 K; liquid nitrogen at 77.36 K. Superconductivity: classical superconductors operate below 10–20 K; high-temperature superconductors below ~135 K. Quantum mechanics: quantum effects dominate at extremely low temperatures near 0 K. Spectroscopy: colour temperature of light sources is expressed in Kelvin (3,000 K = warm white; 6,500 K = daylight).</p>
<h2>Why Negative Temperature Is Impossible (and Sometimes Not)</h2>
<p>In classical thermodynamics, negative Kelvin is impossible because particles cannot have less than zero kinetic energy. However, in quantum systems with an upper energy bound (population inversion, as in lasers), physicists have described states technically "hotter than infinity" — beyond which the scale "wraps around" to negative Kelvin. This paradoxical state (atoms preferentially in high-energy states) is physically real and experimentally achieved, though it does not violate thermodynamics in the classical sense.</p>
<h2>Who Uses Kelvin Daily?</h2>
<p>Physicists in all subfields. Chemists computing reaction equilibria and rates using the Arrhenius equation. Materials scientists studying phase transitions. Astronomers characterising stellar and galactic temperatures. Engineers designing cryogenic systems (superconducting magnets in MRI machines, space telescope cooling). Lighting engineers specifying colour temperature of LEDs and lamps. Refrigeration engineers calculating thermodynamic cycle efficiencies.</p>
<h2>Benefits of the Kelvin Scale</h2>
<ul><li>Enables mathematically correct thermodynamic calculations without arbitrary offsets</li><li>Universal in science — the SI base unit for temperature worldwide</li><li>Natural physical meaning: 0 K = absolute zero = no thermal energy</li><li>Allows direct temperature ratio calculations (e.g., thermodynamic efficiency)</li><li>Connects temperature to energy: average kinetic energy per particle = (3/2)k_B T, where T is in Kelvin</li></ul>
<div class="did-you-know"><h3>Did You Know?</h3><p>Light source colour temperature is measured in Kelvin. A candle flame is approximately 1,800 K (warm orange). An incandescent bulb is 2,700–3,000 K (warm white). Midday sunlight is 5,500–6,500 K (neutral white/daylight). A clear blue sky is 10,000–12,000 K (cool blue). LED lighting products list their colour temperature in Kelvin on packaging.</p></div>`,
