    content: `<p class="lead">Temperature is the most important variable in cooking. The difference between a perfectly medium-rare steak and an overdone one is 5°C (9°F). The difference between safe and unsafe poultry is whether the internal temperature reaches 74°C (165°F). The difference between a properly tempered chocolate and a dull, streaky one is holding at precisely 31–32°C (88–90°F) during final tempering. Professional kitchens worldwide use temperature measurement as a precision tool; home cooks who embrace thermometry produce more consistent results.</p>
<h2>How Cooking Temperature Scales Are Used</h2>
<p>Most cookbooks use the regional convention of their origin: European and Australian recipes use Celsius; US recipes use Fahrenheit. Converting oven temperatures: 180°C = 356°F ≈ Gas Mark 4; 200°C = 392°F ≈ Gas Mark 6; 220°C = 428°F ≈ Gas Mark 7. Internal meat temperatures are expressed in both scales on international recipe platforms. All food safety guidelines worldwide express critical internal temperatures in Celsius in international standards (though US USDA publishes Fahrenheit equivalents).</p>
<h2>Critical Cooking Temperatures</h2>
<p>Food safety: Poultry internal temperature ≥74°C (165°F). Pork, lamb, beef roasts: ≥63°C (145°F). Ground beef/lamb: ≥71°C (160°F). Egg dishes: ≥71°C (160°F). Fish: ≥63°C (145°F). Candy/sugar stages: soft ball 113–116°C (235–240°F); hard crack 149–154°C (300–310°F). Chocolate tempering: melt at 50°C, cool to 27°C, reheat to 31–32°C (dark chocolate). Deep frying: 175–190°C (347–374°F). Bread baking: internal temperature 93–96°C (199–205°F) when done.</p>
<h2>Where Temperature Precision Most Matters in Cooking</h2>
<p>Sous vide (precision cooking): water bath held at exact temperature (e.g., 57°C for 4 hours for medium chicken breast). Chocolate confectionery: tempering at precise temperatures determines crystal structure and sheen. Pastry: butter fat melts at 34°C — above this in pastry dough creates different texture than colder butter. Brewing: yeast fermentation temperature (18–22°C for ales; 8–12°C for lagers) determines flavour profile. Cheesemaking: cultures are added at specific temperatures (32–38°C for most soft cheeses).</p>
<h2>Who Needs Cooking Temperature Knowledge?</h2>
<p>Professional chefs and sous chefs. Pastry chefs and chocolatiers. Home bakers following international recipes. Food safety inspectors verifying restaurant temperature compliance. Food manufacturers setting production parameters. Caterers ensuring safe food at scale. Brewers and winemakers managing fermentation. HACCP (Hazard Analysis Critical Control Points) food safety system operators in all food businesses.</p>
<h2>Benefits of Temperature Precision in Cooking</h2>
<ul><li>Eliminates guesswork, producing consistent results across cooks and kitchens</li><li>Ensures food safety by confirming pathogen-killing temperatures are reached</li><li>Enables precise replication of complex techniques (sous vide, tempering, sugar work)</li><li>Reduces food waste from overcooked or unsafe-to-eat undercooked results</li><li>Required for HACCP food safety compliance in commercial food production</li></ul>
<div class="did-you-know"><h3>Did You Know?</h3><p>Maillard reaction — the browning that creates the crust on bread, seared steak, and roasted coffee — begins at approximately 140–165°C (284–329°F) and accelerates with increasing temperature. The reaction between amino acids and sugars produces hundreds of flavour compounds. This is why a perfectly seared steak cannot be achieved in a sous vide bath alone — surface temperature must reach 140°C+.</p></div>`,
