    content: `<p class="lead">The entire modern world is built from materials whose properties were established at specific temperatures during production. Steel's strength, stainless steel's corrosion resistance, aluminium alloy's hardness, ceramic's density — all are the product of precisely controlled heating and cooling cycles. The temperature control systems that make modern manufacturing possible operate across ranges from room temperature to over 3,000°C, using temperature measurement technologies that must be accurate, reliable, and robust in harsh industrial environments.</p>
<h2>How Temperature Is Controlled in Steel Production</h2>
<p>Steelmaking begins in blast furnaces at approximately 1,500–1,600°C (2,732–2,912°F), where iron ore is reduced to liquid iron. Basic Oxygen Furnaces (BOF) refine liquid iron to steel at approximately 1,600–1,700°C. Electric Arc Furnaces (EAF) melt scrap steel at 1,600°C+. Temperature measurement uses optical pyrometers (non-contact, measuring thermal radiation) and submerged thermocouples. Heat treatment of finished steel — annealing, quenching, tempering — requires precise temperature control to produce desired microstructures. Quenching (rapid cooling from 850°C) produces martensitic (hard) steel; annealing (slow cooling) produces softer, more ductile steel.</p>
<h2>Where Temperature Control Matters in Manufacturing</h2>
<p>Aluminium die casting: melt temperature 660–700°C. Titanium machining: cutting tool temperatures can reach 800°C, requiring careful monitoring to prevent tool damage. Ceramic production: kiln temperatures of 1,200–1,400°C. Glass manufacturing: melting at 1,400–1,500°C; annealing lehr at 500–600°C. Semiconductor manufacturing: CVD (chemical vapour deposition) processes at 300–1,000°C. Additive manufacturing (metal 3D printing): laser melting point temperatures exceeding 1,500°C in micro-spot welding.</p>
<h2>Why Temperature Measurement Technology Matters</h2>
<p>Thermocouples are the workhorse of industrial temperature measurement: two dissimilar metals joined at a tip generate a voltage proportional to the temperature difference between the hot junction and cold junction. Type K thermocouples (Chromel-Alumel) measure −200 to 1,350°C; Type S (Platinum-Rhodium) to 1,600°C; Type B to 1,800°C. Infrared pyrometers enable non-contact measurement of molten metal, rotating kilns, and surfaces in continuous production lines. Fibre optic temperature sensors operate in high-electromagnetic-interference environments like EAF furnaces.</p>
<h2>Who Relies on Industrial Temperature Control?</h2>
<p>Steel mill operators. Aluminium smelter process engineers. Heat treatment shop supervisors. Welding engineers specifying preheat and interpass temperatures. Quality control metallurgists certifying material properties. Ceramic kiln operators. Glass manufacturers. Power plant boiler operators (steam temperature at 540–600°C in modern supercritical boilers). Chemical plant operators managing exothermic reaction temperatures.</p>
<h2>Benefits of Precise Industrial Temperature Control</h2>
<ul><li>Determines the microstructure and mechanical properties of finished metal products</li><li>Required for material certification to international standards (ASTM, ISO, EN)</li><li>Prevents catastrophic equipment failure from thermal overload or thermal shock</li><li>Optimises energy efficiency by avoiding unnecessary over-temperature</li><li>Enables consistent quality in batch and continuous production processes</li></ul>
<div class="did-you-know"><h3>Did You Know?</h3><p>Tungsten, used for incandescent light bulb filaments and high-temperature industrial components, has the highest melting point of any metal at 3,422°C (6,192°F). At this temperature, it glows brilliantly white-hot. Tungsten carbide — an even harder compound — is used in cutting tools that must withstand drilling and milling temperatures exceeding 1,000°C.</p></div>`,
