    content: `<p class="lead">The statement that the world has warmed by approximately 1.2°C above pre-industrial levels is both simple and profound. That single Celsius figure represents the average of thousands of measurements, spanning continents, oceans, and 150 years of instrumental records, processed with sophisticated statistical methods to account for measurement changes, station moves, urban heat islands, and ocean coverage gaps. Understanding what global temperature means, how it is measured, and why fractions of a degree matter is essential for informed engagement with the defining challenge of the 21st century.</p>
<h2>How Global Temperature Is Measured</h2>
<p>The global mean surface temperature (GMST) combines surface air temperature over land from approximately 12,000 weather stations and sea surface temperature from approximately 1,000 voluntary observing ships and buoys. Data from before 1950 relies more heavily on historical records with larger uncertainties. Satellite temperature records (since 1979) provide global coverage of atmospheric temperature at various altitudes. Four major global temperature datasets (NASA GISS, NOAA, UK HadCRUT, and Berkeley Earth) use slightly different methods but produce consistent results. All express anomalies relative to a baseline period (typically 1850–1900 or 1961–1990) in Celsius.</p>
<h2>Why Temperature Anomaly Is Used Instead of Absolute Temperature</h2>
<p>The absolute global average temperature is approximately 14–15°C, but this number has limited scientific utility because the baseline varies with measurement method. Temperature anomaly (the departure from a defined baseline average) is more robust: a station that consistently reads 2°C above its 30-year average tells us something meaningful regardless of whether its absolute average is 10°C or 25°C. Anomalies from different locations can be averaged more reliably than absolute temperatures because systematic biases tend to cancel.</p>
<h2>Where Climate Temperature Data Is Used</h2>
<p>IPCC Assessment Reports synthesising global warming evidence for policymakers. UNFCCC Paris Agreement progress tracking (1.5°C and 2°C warming targets). National adaptation planning (heatwave response, agricultural planning, sea level rise projections). Insurance industry risk assessment for climate-related losses. Financial sector climate stress testing (TCFD disclosures). Military and security planning for climate-driven geopolitical risks.</p>
<h2>Who Works With Climate Temperature Data?</h2>
<p>Climate scientists at research institutions worldwide. Meteorological services maintaining long-term observation records. IPCC report authors and reviewers. Government climate policy advisors. Environmental journalists communicating science to the public. Climate litigators using temperature attribution in legal cases. South African environmental scientists studying regional climate trends affecting rainfall, fire weather, and extreme heat events.</p>
<h2>Benefits of Global Temperature Monitoring</h2>
<ul><li>Provides the evidentiary foundation for international climate agreements and action</li><li>Enables attribution of specific extreme weather events to climate change</li><li>Supports regional climate projections needed for infrastructure planning</li><li>Drives renewable energy investment by quantifying the business case for decarbonisation</li><li>Informs agricultural adaptation to changing growing seasons and drought risk</li></ul>
<div class="did-you-know"><h3>Did You Know?</h3><p>The 1.5°C warming target in the Paris Agreement was chosen because climate models project significantly different risks above this threshold vs below it — including the near-complete loss of coral reefs above 2°C vs 10–30% survival at 1.5°C. The difference between 1.5°C and 2°C of global warming corresponds to approximately 100–150 million more people exposed to extreme heat, flooding, and drought.</p></div>`,
